#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133489 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d893f937-d802-5d27-b91a-b78caee0313e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Norm of a Vector"}] :question/id #uuid "0ab996ae-e3a7-5353-87c2-9b2a01f4f808" :question/math-academy-id "e-5538" :question/problem "Given $\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}$ find $\\|\\mathbf{x}\\|$." :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the norm, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n‖x‖ &= \\sqrt{x_{1}^{2} + x_{2}^{2} + x_{3}^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{3^{2} + (-4)^{2} + (-5)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{9 + 16 + 25} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{50} \\\\\n&= 5\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133490 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff66eef8-74b6-5553-af34-7da5a8cbbb02" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between Two Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "f2ff1620-28e1-59ba-97ca-c43f3f300fdf" :question/math-academy-id "e-5539" :question/problem "Given the vectors $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ find the distance $\\text{d}(\\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y})$." :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the distance, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd(x, y) &= ‖x - y‖ \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(x_{1} - y_{1})^{2} + (x_{2} - y_{2})^{2} + (x_{3} - y_{3})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(-1 - 2)^{2} + (2 - 0)^{2} + (3 - (-1))^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(-3)^{2} + 2^{2} + 4^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{9 + 4 + 16} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{29}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133491 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae41d849-9def-540c-8985-8fbeec679991" :knowledge-point/title "Normalizing a Vector"}] :question/id #uuid "7b7adf29-1653-5de7-915c-80a09f05995a" :question/math-academy-id "e-5540" :question/problem "Normalize the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "To normalize a vector $\\mathbf{x}$, we have to divide the vector by its norm:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathbf{x}}{\\|\\mathbf{x}\\|}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to normalize $\\mathbf{x}$, we first calculate $\\|\\mathbf{x}\\|$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n‖x‖ &= \\sqrt{x_{1}^{2} + x_{2}^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(-8)^{2} + 6^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{64 + 36} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{100} \\\\\n&= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide $\\mathbf{x}$ by its length:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x}{‖x‖} &= \\frac{x}{10} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{10}x \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{-8}{10} \\\\ \\frac{6}{10}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133562 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "837e0fa4-b8d3-59c1-87c7-44317adafaf9" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Constant Out of the Norm"}] :question/id #uuid "86cf381d-8a69-5290-806b-09477c7ff92e" :question/math-academy-id "e-6190" :question/problem "If $\\left\\Vert {-\\dfrac{\\mathbf{x}}{2}} \\right\\Vert = 5$, find $\\Vert 3 \\mathbf{x} \\Vert$." :question/worked-solution "Remember that we can factor a constant out of the norm, provided that we take the absolute value of the constant:\n\n$$\n\\Vert k \\mathbf{x} \\Vert= \\vert k \\vert \\cdot \\Vert \\mathbf{x} \\Vert\n$$\n\nUsing this fact, we can solve for the norm $\\Vert x \\Vert$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n‖- \\frac{x}{2}‖ &= 5 \\\\\n‖- \\frac{1}{2}x‖ &= 5 \\\\\n\\mid - \\frac{1}{2} \\mid \\cdot ‖x‖ &= 5 \\\\\n\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot ‖x‖ &= 5 \\\\\n‖x‖ &= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can evaluate $\\Vert 3 \\mathbf{x} \\Vert$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n‖3x‖ &= 3 \\cdot ‖x‖ \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot 10 \\\\\n&= 30\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134126 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d893f937-d802-5d27-b91a-b78caee0313e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Norm of a Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #u